<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Transportation – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Transportation</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Rules of the Road</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend discussions on familiar subjects. Interactive: Contribute to instructions in workshops.
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can comprehend discussions on road safety rules (e.g., "Identify why wearing a seatbelt is life-saving." Using crosswalks in busy areas, like Avenida Balboa, is important to prevent an accident." Understand why using the sidewalk or staying out of the road is essential when walking near unlit roads in rural areas.")
            Can follow and contribute to instructions in workshops about public transportation (e.g., "You should walk to the bus stop, then take bus number ten to get to the shopping mall.")
            Can follow instructions for a group activity on transportation options (e.g., "I think using the bus is more cost-effective than taking a taxi because it's cheaper.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing pictures of various transportation methods in Panama (e.g., Diablo Rojo bus, Metro, taxi, a person walking on a sidewalk, a car on a highway). Students listen and identify them. The teacher asks: "How do you usually travel to school?" "What do you see in these pictures?" "What happens on the *road*?" This introduces core vocabulary like *bus, car, road, sidewalk, passenger, travel*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words, focusing on stress in multisyllabic words: "transporTAtion," "conVEnience," "SCHEdule," "PASSenger," "VEhicle," "HIGHway." The teacher also models the distinction between long and short vowels in minimal pairs: /æ/ in "passenger" versus /ɑː/ in "port," and /æ/ in "tap" versus /eɪ/ in "tape." The teacher demonstrates short sentences using modal verbs for advice: "You *should* wear a seatbelt." "You *have to* stop at a red light." and phrasal verbs: "You *get on* the bus at the stop."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the introduced vocabulary and concepts.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you are a *passenger* in a car, do you drive?" (No, the driver drives. You are the person riding.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do you *have to* stop at a green traffic light?" (No, you *have to* stop at a red light.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a *sidewalk* for cars or for people walking?" (It's for people walking to keep them safe.)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p>___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Transportation")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students will listen to a short audio clip (approx. 1.5 minutes) which is a public service announcement (PSA) about road safety rules for pedestrians and public transportation passengers. The genre is an informative radio announcement, with a semi-formal register. The format is an audio recording. It will include phrases like: "You *must* wear a *seatbelt*," "Always use a *crosswalk*," "Pedestrians *should* stay on the *sidewalk*," and "Remember to *tap in* when you *board* the *bus*."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend discussions on familiar subjects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the audio twice.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question (1st Listening):</b> "What is the main topic of this announcement? Is it about food, school, or road safety and transportation?" (Expected answer: Road safety and transportation).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (2nd Listening):</b> Students listen again and mark the following statements as True (T) or False (F) on a worksheet.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">You don't need a seatbelt if you are in the back seat. (F)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">It is important to use crosswalks in busy areas. (T)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Walking in the road is safer than walking on the sidewalk. (F)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">You should tap in when you get on the bus. (T)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p>___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice listening for modal verbs for advice and obligation (*should, have to, must*), comparatives (*faster, safest, cheapest*), and vocabulary related to transportation (*passenger, seatbelt, traffic light, sidewalk, bus, train, taxi, route, fare, schedule, convenience, safety*).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Listening Cloze:</b> The teacher plays parts of the PSA again, pausing at key points. Students listen and fill in the blanks in sentences on their worksheet with target vocabulary or modal verbs.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"All ______ have to wear their ______." (passengers, seatbelts)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"You ______ stop at a red ______." (have to, traffic light)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It is ______ to stay on the ______ when walking." (safest, sidewalk)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"To avoid a ______, you ______ check the bus ______." (delay, should, schedule)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Listen and Choose Advice:</b> The teacher reads short scenarios aloud. Students listen and choose the best advice using a modal verb or a comparative from two options provided on their worksheet.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher reads: "Maria needs to go from Albrook to Multiplaza quickly during rush hour. She could take a bus or a taxi."
                                <br>Students choose: A) She *should* take the bus because it's *cheaper*. B) She *should* take the Metro because it's *faster* and avoids *traffic* delays.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher reads: "Pedro is walking near a busy street at night with no lights."
                                <br>Students choose: A) He *must* walk on the *sidewalk*. B) He *should* walk in the middle of the *road*.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Pair Check - Listen and Confirm:</b> In pairs, one student reads a sentence about a transportation rule or option, and the other student listens carefully and confirms if they agree, or gently corrects if there's a mistake, using the target language. This practices attentive listening and immediate response.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "I think taking the bus is *more convenient* because you don't have to *drive*."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "Yes, I agree. Taking the bus is often *more convenient*."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "You *should* cross the street anywhere you want."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "No, you *should* use the *crosswalk* for *safety*."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p>___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Transportation")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Discussion and Presentation based on listening.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher plays 3-4 short, distinct audio clips (e.g., a short traffic report, a warning from a bus driver, a snippet of a discussion about public transport *benefits* and *delays*). Each clip focuses on a different aspect of "Rules of the Road" or "Transportation Options."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Groups *listen* to all clips. Their task is to collaboratively *discuss* and identify the <b>TOP 3 Most Important Road Safety Rules or Transportation Tips</b> heard in the audio clips that are relevant for people in Panama. They must explain *why* each rule/tip is important, using modal verbs (*should, must, have to*) for advice/obligation and comparatives (*safer, more convenient, cheaper*) to justify their choices. Students *listen* to each other's suggestions and agree on their group's top three. Finally, each group orally presents their "Top 3" to the class, making sure each member contributes by speaking about at least one point, emphasizing *safety* in *travel* and *convenience* of *transportation* choices. For example: "We listened to the report, and we think using your *seatbelt* is the *safest* rule, you *must* always wear it." "For *convenience*, taking the Metro is *faster* than a car during *rush hour*, you *should* try it."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion, primarily demonstrated through their ability to synthesize information from listening and discuss effectively.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p>___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during group discussion and oral presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' group presentation of their "TOP 3 Road Safety Rules/Transportation Tips" and their justifications, demonstrating comprehension from the audio input and collaborative interaction. The teacher observes individual contributions within the group.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies key safety rules/instructions accurately from listening input.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least 3 target vocabulary words (e.g., *seatbelt, sidewalk, traffic, bus, passenger*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Applies modal verbs for advice/obligation correctly (e.g., *should, must, have to*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Applies comparatives and superlatives (e.g., *faster, safest, more convenient*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Contributes to group discussion and listens to peers' ideas.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation is generally intelligible, especially for multisyllabic words and targeted vowel sounds.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts individually:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was one important road safety rule you clearly understood from listening today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was something challenging about listening to other students' ideas or the audio clips?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p>___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How well did I understand the different rules and tips about transportation and safety today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is one way I can improve my listening skills for understanding instructions or advice?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson to engage students and promote listening comprehension? The use of authentic audio clips and group discussion seemed effective in encouraging active listening and negotiation of meaning.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled with distinguishing between *should* and *must* for obligation strength; this needs more practice. Perhaps provide more visual cues alongside audio for lower-level listeners. The pacing for the listening activities might need to be adjusted to allow for more processing time.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 2), students will reinforce the vocabulary and concepts introduced today by reading a short text about "Rules of the Road" and public transportation, focusing on reading comprehension strategies.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p>___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>